  "Every epigraph (KJV in the English) is Louis Segond (1910) verbatim with French typographic spacing, and the “(KJV)” label is dropped; the attribution names Segond accordingly. Partial verses where the English is partial: John 1:12 stops before Segond’s run-on « lesquels sont nés, » (and Segond’s « l’ont reçue » refers to the Word/light, feminine); Acts 1:8 stops at « mes témoins »; Hebrews 12:2 is the opening clause with its first letter capitalised.",
  "Psalm 55:17 (English numbering) is Psaume 55:18 in Segond; the epigraph cites « Psaume 55:18 » and the reference row keeps the English number. Segond’s wording (« je soupire et je gémis ») differs from the KJV’s “pray, and cry aloud” but is the verse.",
  "Torrey’s own sentences are quoted in my own French rendering (no published French edition of How to Succeed in the Christian Life is on the site); his “He/Him” for God/Christ is lower-cased per house style. Ch. 6 « Suis-moi » is marked mined against Matthew 9:9 (Segond’s exact words); Torrey may have had John 21:22 in mind.",
  "Title « La foi qui tient (Pour ados) » is exactly 28 characters: the literal « Réussir sa vie chrétienne (Pour ados) » is 37 and too long for the rail cover, so the title carries the book’s ‘keep going / succeed’ idea; the literal French gloss appears in ch. 1 and the closing line of ch. 12, and the English title is kept in the attribution and the Q&A (no French full edition exists). Teen voice uses « tu », narration of Torrey’s life in passé simple, as a-retrospect-teens.fr does.",
  "AI-translated in-session; awaiting native review."
